<%#
  THE VISITOR'S HEADER (stage 4, S4-5; story 5.7). The anonymous branch of the layout carried no
  chrome at all: a visitor who landed on a project page had nothing naming the site and no way to
  the maker's other work but the breadcrumb.

  RENDERED ONLY ON A PUBLIC SURFACE, never on the auth card. Devise's pages share the anonymous
  branch and are a different surface — a sign-in form under an Explore link reads as a site nav on
  a page that has none. `public_shell?` is the same question `shell_signed_in?` asks first, and it
  is ONE definition (ShellHelper) rather than a second copy here.

  NO SESSION, THEREFORE NO ACCOUNT LINK. This partial renders identically for a signed-in owner
  previewing their own public page (Public::BaseController#public_surface? is true whatever the
  session says), which is the honest preview; adding "signed in as…" here would be the session read
  §14d forbids on this surface. It lives under app/views/layouts/ rather than app/views/public/, so
  the seam guard's Public scan does not reach it — what keeps it honest is that it names no session
  helper at all: `public_shell?` and the two ivars the public controllers set.

  `.brand` IS THE RAIL'S OWN MARKUP, reused rather than restyled — shell.css defines it once.
%>
<header class="public-header">
  <%= link_to public_explore_path, class: "brand", "aria-label": "Tinkerex — explore" do %>
    <span class="brand-mark" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="brand-name">Tinkerex</span>
  <% end %>
  <%# THE AUTHOR, when the page has one. @owner on /u/:handle, @pub on a project page — both are
      set by the controller that renders them, and `defined?` is what keeps this partial usable on
      /explore, which has neither. %>
  <% author = (@pub&.handle if defined?(@pub)) || (@owner&.handle if defined?(@owner)) %>
  <%# A public page that is not about published projects names itself instead (the compose
      generator does), through `content_for :public_intro`; every other page keeps the line. %>
  <% if author %>
    <%= link_to "@#{author}", public_user_path(author), class: "public-author" %>
  <% else %>
    <p class="public-intro muted"><%= content_for(:public_intro).presence || "Projects makers have published — the files, the photos and the build log." %></p>
  <% end %>
</header>
